   230	def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
   231	    """
   232	    Add a size-driven halo whose geometry and depth are both encoded by each foreground component's pixel count.
   233	
   234	    Input structure:
   235	    - The grid is a black background with several isolated non-black 4-connected foreground components.
   236	    - Each component is monochrome in valid inputs, has area 3-7, and keeps its original color.
   237	    - Components are spaced so their derived halo regions normally do not overlap.
   238	
   239	    Rule:
   240	    - Find every 4-connected non-black component and count its pixels as N.
   241	    - If N is even, grow N/2 diagonal-only layers from the component and mark those reachable cells yellow.
   242	    - If N is odd, grow (N-1)/2 orthogonal-only layers from the component and mark those reachable cells orange.
   243	    - Skip out-of-bounds cells, never overwrite original foreground pixels, and if halo regions overlap then the larger-N component takes priority.
   244	
   245	    Key insight:
   246	    - The same quantity N is used twice: parity chooses the neighborhood geometry, while the magnitude chooses how many expansion layers are drawn.
   247	    """
